meta-avocado-sbom
=================

Produces the package to CVE report that avocado-cli downloads to tell a user
which CVEs affect the packages installed in their image.

Sources
-------

Two artifacts a world build leaves in TMPDIR, both persistent across builds:

  ${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/world-recipe-sbom.sbom-cve-check.yocto.json
                                          CVE analysis of the world SBOM
  ${TMPDIR}/pkgdata/*/runtime-reverse/*   runtime package name -> recipe

The first is written by sbom-cve-check. wrynose OE-core has no cve-check class
any more: the sbom-cve-check tool reads an SPDX 3.0 document, matches its
purl/CPE identifiers against the cvelistV5 and NVD databases, applies the VEX
statements in that same document, and exports the result as a manifest in the
JSON shape the old class used to write per recipe. This layer appends
sbom-cve-check-recipe to OE-core's meta-world-recipe-sbom, whose SBOM covers
every world target, so one scan describes the whole build. DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE is
already ${DEPLOY_DIR}/images/${MACHINE}, so nothing has to be scoped by hand.

The two are joined on the recipe name. CVEs are stored once per recipe and
packages only reference it, so a recipe shipping many packages does not
duplicate its CVE list.

Requirements
------------

  kas build kas/machine/<machine>.yml:kas/feature/cve-check.yml

That fragment carries this layer, raises SPDX_INCLUDE_VEX to "all" so the scan
can see the Patched and Ignored statements, and points the CVE database recipes
at AUTOREV. kas/feature/sbom.yml is the SPDX fragment feed builds stack:
stacking it as well is harmless, but this report needs the cve-check one.

Outside kas, with this layer in bblayers.conf, the equivalent in local.conf:

  SPDX_INCLUDE_VEX = "all"
  SRCREV:pn-sbom-cve-check-update-nvd-native = "${AUTOREV}"
  SRCREV:pn-sbom-cve-check-update-cvelist-native = "${AUTOREV}"

plus the four kernel lines under "Kernel CVEs" below.

create-spdx does not have to be inherited by hand: OE-core does it through
INHERIT_DISTRO, and there create-spdx is create-spdx-3.0.

SPDX_INCLUDE_VEX has to be in place before the build that produces the SPDX
documents, not just before this recipe runs.

Usage
-----

  bitbake world
  bitbake avocado-cve-report

Writes ${DEPLOY_DIR}/avocado-cve/avocado-cve-report-${MACHINE}.json.

World scope needs every world recipe to resolve. A plain machine build does
not carry the vendor layers some world recipes need (kas/feature/complete.yml
adds them), and a recipe whose runtime dependency has no provider stops the
whole scan at the first one it meets. OE-core's WORLD_SBOM_EXCLUDE lists
recipes to leave out of the world SBOM.

On wrynose, world scope is not supported for a single machine build. On
raspberrypi5 the world SBOM stops at, in order: avocado-sdk-scripts
(packagegroup-avocado-container, COMPATIBLE_MACHINE avocado-container),
wpewebkit (packagegroup-avocado-feature-graphics), greengrass-bin
(packagegroup-avocado-feature-cloud-aws), gst-plugin-pylon
(packagegroup-avocado-feature-cameras), gnome-settings-daemon (budgie-desktop),
python3-pkg-resources (azote) and cups-pk-helper. The first four are Avocado
packagegroups whose provider layer is absent; the rest are meta-wayland
recipes broken on wrynose, so excluding the packagegroups would not make the
scan resolve. Bitbake names one recipe per run, so WORLD_SBOM_EXCLUDE becomes a
list to maintain. Use image scope on wrynose.

Image scope works on any machine build, with no extra layers. It scans the SBOM
of one image, the packages that image installs:

  kas build kas/machine/<machine>.yml:kas/feature/cve-check-image.yml \
      --target avocado-cve-report

That fragment sets AVOCADO_CVE_SCOPE = "image" and adds sbom-cve-check to
IMAGE_CLASSES; AVOCADO_CVE_IMAGE names the image, avocado-image-rootfs by
default. The report format is the same, and pkgdata still describes every
package the build made, so counts.packages is build-wide while the CVEs are the
image's. Use the world scan for "what could a user install from the feed" and
this one for "what does this image ship".

do_cve_report depends on meta-world-recipe-sbom:do_sbom_cve_check_recipe, so
the second command runs the scan itself. The world build is still needed first,
for pkgdata: the scan alone never writes it.

The same code can run standalone against a build tree, if needed:

  PYTHONPATH=meta-avocado-sbom/lib python3 -m avocado_sbom.report \
      --tmpdir build/tmp --machine <machine> -o avocado-cve-report.json

A later scan of the same build's SBOM, with newer CVE data, can be turned into
a report without the build tree. The previous report stands in for pkgdata:

  PYTHONPATH=meta-avocado-sbom/lib python3 -m avocado_sbom.report \
      --from-report avocado-cve-report-<machine>.json \
      --manifest <image>.sbom-cve-check.yocto.json -o avocado-cve-report.json

The result is byte for byte the report this layer would have written for that
manifest, apart from "generated": "packages", "recipe_versions",
packages_digest, pkgdata_unreadable, package_collisions, machine and
distro_version carry over, and the status does too unless --status is given.
--no-summary is not recorded in a report, so pass it again if the previous
report was written with it. A report without "recipe_versions" is refused,
since matching against the packages' versions loses CVEs (see "Report
format"), as is one whose packages do not hash to its packages_digest.
build_report_from_anchor() in avocado_sbom/report.py is the same as a
function.

Kernel CVEs
-----------

sbom-cve-check filters kernel CVEs on configuration by itself. A CVE whose
affected files (the kernel CNA's programFiles, in cvelistV5) are none of the
files the kernel compiled is not affected, "Source code not compiled by
config". The scanner takes that file list from the hasInput sources of the
kernel's build in the SPDX document, and trusts it only if it names
init/main.c, include/linux/module.h or a *.mod.c file. Otherwise it warns that
the list is "missing important files" and matches the kernel on version ranges
alone.

OE-core writes the list when SPDX_INCLUDE_COMPILED_SOURCES is "1" for the
kernel recipe, from the sources the kernel's debug info names, so the kernel
has to carry debug info. kas/feature/sbom.yml sets:

  AVOCADO_KERNEL_DEBUGINFO = "1"
  SPDX_INCLUDE_COMPILED_SOURCES:pn-linux-yocto = "1"
  SPDX_INCLUDE_COMPILED_SOURCES:pn-linux-yocto-dev = "1"
  SPDX_INCLUDE_COMPILED_SOURCES:pn-linux-raspberrypi = "1"

The switch changes the kernel's packages, so it is in sbom.yml, which feed
builds stack: a scan has to see the kernel the feed published, or the kernel's
RPM digests match nothing in the feed. kas/feature/cve-check.yml repeats the
lines for a scan stacked without sbom.yml. The SPDX lines are :pn-scoped
because the variable also turns on SPDX_INCLUDE_SOURCES for the recipe, which
tree-wide would list every source file of every recipe.

avocado-kernel-feed.bbclass reads the switch for linux-yocto and
linux-yocto-dev and adds features/debug/debug-kernel.scc to KERNEL_FEATURES.
The bbappend in meta-avocado-raspberrypi adds avocado-cve-debug.cfg to
linux-raspberrypi on the same switch. The DWARF lands in kernel-dbg; what a
device installs stays stripped. linux-yocto-rt, the L4T kernels and the vendor
kernels are left as they are, and the scan matches them on version ranges.

What the switch does to each machine's kernel configuration:

  no change, debug info is          amd-x86-64-v4, arm64-uefi-v9, the Jetson
  already on                        machines' linux-yocto, nanopi-r5s,
                                    orangepi-5-plus
  adds debug info only              intel-x86-64-v2/v3/v4 (DEBUG_KERNEL from
                                    x86_64_defconfig), the Raspberry Pi
                                    machines (EXPERT in the bcm27xx defconfigs
                                    selects DEBUG_KERNEL)
  DEBUG_KERNEL is off without it    qemux86-64, qemuarm64

On the QEMU machines, turning DEBUG_KERNEL on also un-gates runtime debug
options that their BSP's kernel-cache fragments request or that default on
under it: KGDB and KDB with their console hooks, CGROUP_DEBUG, RCU_TRACE,
DEBUG_MISC and X86_DEBUG_FPU. meta-avocado-qemu's linux-yocto bbappend sets
AVOCADO_KERNEL_DEBUGINFO_RUNTIME_OFF = "1" for its machines, which adds
avocado/debug-kernel-runtime-off.scc (meta-avocado/files/avocado-kmeta) after
every other feature and turns them back off. DEBUG_KERNEL itself stays on,
since debug info depends on it, and the BPF verifier tests it directly: an
internal verifier error also raises a kernel warning there. A layer whose
linux-yocto machine has DEBUG_KERNEL off without the switch should set
AVOCADO_KERNEL_DEBUGINFO_RUNTIME_OFF for that machine too.

The Raspberry Pi defconfigs install modules xz-compressed, and OE-core only
splits and strips files named .ko. With the switch on, the bbappend unpacks the
modules in the packaging copy (PKGD) and compresses them again, with the
kernel's xz options, once they are split, so their DWARF goes to kernel-dbg and
their sources reach the list. The packages and the deploy modules tarball keep
.ko.xz.

A build with the module-signing distro feature keeps linux-yocto and its SPDX
as they are: OE-core does not split or strip a signed module either, so its
sources would be missing from the list and a CVE in it would read as not
compiled.

Only image scope gets this filtering. The world SBOM is built from recipe
documents, which describe no build and so carry no compiled-source list.

Feed scope
----------

Feed scope scans the whole package feed as one unit, the packages a user could
install, without needing every world recipe to resolve. Stack the fragment with
the SBOM and CVE fragments, build the feed, then run the report:

  kas build kas/machine/<machine>.yml:kas/feature/sbom.yml:kas/feature/cve-check.yml:kas/feature/cve-check-feed.yml \
      --target avocado-cve-report

The fragment is kas/feature/cve-check-feed.yml. It sets AVOCADO_CVE_SCOPE =
"feed" and nothing else. Run bitbake avocado-cve-report as its own invocation
after the feed build has finished, not in the same command as the feed target:
do_feed_sbom has no dependency on packaging, so run together it could assemble
the document while packages are still being written. It runs do_feed_sbom and
then the scan. Three files come out in AVOCADO_FEED_SBOM_DIR, which defaults to
the directory of the CVE report and the RPM manifest
(${DEPLOY_DIR}/avocado-cve, that is tmp/deploy/avocado-cve), named by
MACHINE_SHORT_NAME, the MACHINE without its avocado- prefix (raspberrypi5 for
avocado-raspberrypi5):

  avocado-feed-<MACHINE_SHORT_NAME>.spdx.json
      the feed SPDX document, built from every recipe that shipped a package
  avocado-feed-<short>.sbom-cve-check.yocto.json
      the scanner manifest that avocado-cve-report reads at this scope
  avocado-feed-<short>.coverage.json
      the coverage record, with the keys roots, no_document, unresolved_ids,
      alt_included and alt_not_covered. roots counts the recipe documents in the
      feed document, alternate kernels included, so roots plus no_document is
      not the number of recipes that shipped.

A recipe that shipped a package but has no recipe-level SPDX document is named
in no_document and listed as unscanned in the report. On a Raspberry Pi 5 feed
344 of 367 recipes had a document, so 23 were unscanned.
AVOCADO_FEED_SBOM_MAX_MISSING is the ceiling of recipes without a document
before do_feed_sbom fails; it defaults to 33. The list of what shipped is the
build's package data, so it must come from the same build tree as the documents:
a package data directory shortened by another bitbake invocation shortens the
scan without a trace.

The task fails closed. An unresolved SPDX identifier, a missing build or more
missing recipes than the ceiling fails do_feed_sbom and leaves no document and
no manifest, so a partial scan is never mistaken for a complete one. A scan
whose manifest is missing or is not JSON removes the document and record too.

The files stay out of the image deploy directory on purpose. The bootfiles
package (avocado-img-bootfiles) collects every file in that directory whose name
its skip patterns do not match, so a feed document or manifest left there would
be built into an RPM of the feed. The document carries upstream VEX statements,
so it is scan input only and is never published to customers; python3 -m
avocado_sbom.feedcheck fails if an avocado-feed- file sits in the image deploy
directory or a bootfiles RPM lists one.

Alternate kernels are read from AVOCADO_MULTIKERNEL_MC_RECIPES, a list of
<multiconfig>:<recipe> entries. Each alternate multiconfig's documents are
loaded from its own TMPDIR, tmp-<multiconfig>. A kernel that passes is listed in
alt_included as "<multiconfig>:<recipe>". A kernel is listed in alt_not_covered
with a reason instead when a merge would point a vulnerability statement at the
wrong kernel (reference collision), when it shares an identity alias with the
default kernel that a statement or relationship refers to (alias collision),
or when its document is missing. A malformed list entry is listed there too.
The alternate kernels' packages stay out of the RPM manifest and the report's
package map. A kernel in alt_included is in the scanned document and the scanner
manifest, but the CVE report lists only versions the build's own package data
holds, so an alternate kernel at another version is counted as scanned and its
CVEs are not in the report. Links from an alternate kernel's document to a recipe
that only its multiconfig built do not resolve, and do_feed_sbom then fails on the
unresolved identifier instead of moving that kernel to alt_not_covered.

Variables
---------

  AVOCADO_CVE_SCOPE            "world" (default), "image" or "feed"; see Usage
                               and Feed scope
  AVOCADO_FEED_SBOM_MAX_MISSING  ceiling of recipes without an SPDX document
                               before do_feed_sbom fails, default 33
  AVOCADO_FEED_SBOM_DIR        where the feed document, manifest and coverage
                               record go, default the CVE report directory; never
                               the image deploy directory
  AVOCADO_CVE_IMAGE            image scanned at image scope, avocado-image-rootfs
  AVOCADO_CVE_MANIFEST         sbom-cve-check manifest to read, or a directory
                               of them; defaults to the world SBOM's export in
                               ${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}, or the image's at image scope
  AVOCADO_CVE_REPORT_FILE      output path
  AVOCADO_CVE_REPORT_STATUS    CVE status to report, default "Unpatched".
                               Set to "Ignored" to audit what
                               cve-extra-exclusions.inc and any local
                               CVE_STATUS declaration suppress; each issue then
                               carries "detail" (the CVE_STATUS keyword -
                               cpe-incorrect is a false positive,
                               upstream-wontfix is not) and "description" (the
                               maintainer's rationale), which is the whole
                               point of running that mode. Both reach the
                               manifest as the VEX status notes and statement.
                               "Patched" is the mode that needs
                               SPDX_INCLUDE_VEX = "all": under "current" the
                               fixed-version and cpe-stable-backport statements
                               never reach the document to be reported on.
                               "All" lists every status in one report and puts
                               a "status" on each CVE, so a fix shows up as a
                               CVE moving from Unpatched to Patched between
                               builds and Patched, Ignored and absent stay
                               distinguishable. At "All", counts.unpatched_cves,
                               patched_cves and ignored_cves count what the
                               report lists, so they add up to counts.cves; at
                               any single status they count the CVEs of the
                               other statuses left out of it. When two
                               manifests name one recipe, a CVE id is listed
                               once, with the status of the first manifest
  AVOCADO_CVE_REPORT_SUMMARY   "0" drops CVE description text
  AVOCADO_RPM_MANIFEST_FILE    output path of the RPM manifest, see "The RPM
                               manifest" below

Report format
-------------

  {
    "version": "1",
    "generated": "2026-07-31T12:00:00Z",
    "machine": "avocado-qemux86-64",
    "packages_digest": "sha256:0f3c...",
    "status": "Unpatched",
    "unscanned_recipes": [ "packagegroup-core-boot", "..." ],
    "counts": { "recipes": 225, "cves": 2206,
                "packaged_recipes": 177, "packaged_cves": 1927,
                "packages": 30747, "cve_files": 1, "stale_dropped": 0,
                "unscanned_recipes": 34, "cve_files_unreadable": 0,
                "entries_unreadable": 0, "pkgdata_unreadable": 0,
                "package_collisions": 0, "unpatched_cves": 0,
                "patched_cves": 0, "ignored_cves": 0,
                "unknown_status_cves": 0 },
    "recipes": {
      "openssl": {
        "version": "3.5.7",
        "packaged": true,
        "cves": [ { "id": "CVE-...", "scorev3": "7.5", "vector": "NETWORK",
                    "summary": "...", "link": "https://nvd.nist.gov/..." } ]
      }
    },
    "packages": {
      "libssl3": { "recipe": "openssl", "version": "3.5.7-r0.0",
                   "origin": "avocado-qemux86-64" }
    }
  }

"packaged": false marks a recipe absent from pkgdata, i.e. one that produced no
runtime package. Two different things land there:

  - -native and -cross recipes, which run on the build host and correlate to
    nothing installed. They are kept because they still describe the build's
    own exposure.
  - deploy-only target recipes, which do ship on the device but outside any
    package: imx-atf and imx-boot (do_install[noexec], inherit deploy),
    u-boot variants with PACKAGES = "", optee-os, firmware-* recipes. Their
    CVEs are real and on the device.
  - recipes this build no longer produces at all. The manifest is rewritten
    whole by each scan, so unlike scarthgap's per-recipe ${DEPLOY_DIR}/cve
    files these cannot pile up across configuration changes - but the world
    SBOM behind it is only as current as the last do_create_recipe_sbom, and
    stale_dropped only fires when pkgdata knows the recipe at some other
    version.

  cve_files             manifests consumed; one, unless AVOCADO_CVE_MANIFEST
                        points at a directory holding several
  unscanned_recipes     recipes that shipped packages but are absent from the
                        manifest - unscanned, not clean. Named individually in
                        the top-level "unscanned_recipes" list, so a consumer
                        can tell a recipe nothing examined from one that was
                        examined and found clean; both are absent from
                        "recipes". Normally non-zero: every recipe that
                        inherits nospdx is absent from the world SBOM and so
                        from the scan, which on wrynose is all 32 packagegroups
                        plus the SDK metadata recipes. A recipe that was
                        scanned and came back clean is not here - it has an
                        entry in the manifest with an empty issue list. Watch
                        for a sharp rise, which means the world SBOM covered
                        less than the packages pkgdata knows about - typically
                        a world build that stopped early
  stale_dropped         manifest entries discarded because no recipe in
                        pkgdata built that version. Their recipes still count
                        as scanned, so they are not in "unscanned_recipes" as
                        well - a version mismatch and a recipe nothing looked
                        at stay distinguishable
  cve_files_unreadable  manifests that would not parse, including files that
                        are valid JSON of the wrong shape
  entries_unreadable    single manifest entries of the wrong shape, skipped
                        one by one; the rest of that manifest is still read
  unknown_status_cves   CVEs filtered out on a status that is not one of
                        Unpatched, Patched or Ignored - nothing sbom-cve-check
                        is known to emit, so a non-zero value means the report
                        is not describing everything the scan found. The
                        bitbake task warns on it
  pkgdata_unreadable    pkgdata entries that would not parse
  package_collisions    package names claimed by more than one recipe or
                        version; the first pkgdata directory won

"recipe_versions" maps every recipe in pkgdata, scanned clean or not, to its
packages' PV and plain PKGV, sorted and without repeats:

  "recipe_versions": { "glibc": [ "2.43+git", "2.43+git1+562cd7badd" ] }

A package's own PKGV:<package> (lvm2's libdevmapper at 1.02.213) is left out:
no manifest entry carries it. These are the versions a manifest entry is
matched against: an entry for a recipe listed here is dropped as stale when
neither its version nor that version with any EXTENDPE prefix ("1_1.20.0")
stripped is one of them, and an entry for a recipe not listed is kept. A
consumer rebuilding the report from a later scan without the build tree needs
the map to reach the same CVEs: "packages" carries PKGV-PKGR, which is not
what a manifest entry records, and matching against it drops every recipe
whose PV differs from it, as glibc's does. packages_digest does not cover the
map, and it is optional and additive, so the report stays version 1.

The RPM manifest
----------------

  bitbake avocado-cve-report

also writes ${DEPLOY_DIR}/avocado-cve/avocado-rpm-manifest-${MACHINE}.json:
every RPM the build produced, each with the digest of its header. A service
that keeps a verdict per build needs it, because two builds of one
name-version-release are different files with different headers, and neither
Pulp nor the report records which build a file came from. It joins the report
on the package name and a repository on the header digest. The format is the
one scarthgap writes, so one consumer reads both.

  {
    "version": "1",
    "generated": "2026-10-22T19:38:57Z",
    "machine": "avocado-raspberrypi5",
    "distro_version": "2026.0+801.ge5a9500e",
    "packages": [
      { "name": "avocadoctl", "epoch": null, "version": "0.14.0",
        "release": "r0.0", "arch": "cortexa76",
        "sha256header": "<64 lowercase hex>",
        "file": "cortexa76/avocadoctl-0.14.0-r0.0.cortexa76.rpm" }
    ]
  }

Packages are sorted by name, arch, version and release. epoch is an integer, or
null when the package has none; zero is an epoch. sha256header is the sha256 of
the RPM's main header section - rpm's SHA256HEADER, and the byte range Pulp
reports as rpm_header_start to rpm_header_end - not a digest of the whole file,
which also covers the payload. file is relative to ${DEPLOY_DIR_RPM}. version
has a "+" where the upstream version has a "-", because rpm refuses a hyphen
there.

It lists everything ${PKGDATA_DIR} names, the -dbg, -dev and -src packages
included, not only what an image installs: a service rescanning the whole feed,
and customer runtimes that pull packages the reference image does not have,
need all of it. This holds at both report scopes. Pkgdata says which version
and release of each package this build made, and ${DEPLOY_DIR_RPM} keeps every
release ever built, so each file is named from pkgdata's PKGV-PKGR rather than
globbed, and takes its arch from the directory it sits in. A package with its
own PKGV (lvm2's libdevmapper) is named by that one. The header found there
must then name the same package.

It reads the same machine pkgdata as the report, because the two are consumed
as a pair and a manifest naming a package the report lacks is rejected
downstream. So on a machine that builds a second kernel in another multiconfig
(AVOCADO_MULTIKERNEL_MC_RECIPES), only the default multiconfig's RPMs are
listed: the report does not read tmp-<mc> pkgdata, and the task notes this in
the build log. SDK pkgdata is not listed either.

A package pkgdata names that holds no files (PKGSIZE 0) and has no RPM is not
listed and is not a failure: package_rpm writes no RPM for an empty package
unless ALLOW_EMPTY, while pkgdata still names it - avocado-core-dbg reads this
way. An empty package that does have an RPM, a packagegroup, is listed. A
package whose size pkgdata does not state is not excused this way.

Any other package with no RPM depends on whether the scanned image ships it:

  the image ships it           the build fails
  the image does not ship it   left out, with a build warning that names it

At image scope pkgdata also names packages the image does not install, some
built only as build-time dependencies, which may have no RPM. Those are on no
device, so leaving them out costs nothing, and every name that is listed is
still in the report's "packages". A package the image does install must be
listed, so a missing RPM for it is a failure: it means pkgdata names a version
whose RPM was never written, as when another target's build refreshes a
dependency's pkgdata at a new PKGR. "Ships" is read from the image's
installed-package manifest in ${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}, the one write_image_manifest
writes beside the image; if it cannot be read, the task warns and treats every
package as shipped.

At world scope there is no image to ask, and the feed publishes everything the
world build packaged, so every package with no RPM fails the build, as on
scarthgap.

Anything else wrong with a package fails the build, with every such package
named: a version with no release, an arch that is ambiguous, a file whose
header names something else or will not read as an RPM, or one whose signature
header records a different SHA256HEADER than its main header hashes to. The
previous build's file is removed first, so a failure leaves no list at all.

The task runs after do_cve_report. At image scope that means after the image:
do_cve_report waits for the image's do_sbom_cve_check, which follows the
rootfs, and do_rootfs waits for the do_package_write_rpm of everything it
installs. At world scope nothing in that chain waits for packaging, because the
world SBOM is built from recipe-level SPDX, so run it after the world build has
finished; a package still being packaged then fails the task rather than being
left out.

The same limit as the report's counters applies: pkgdata and ${DEPLOY_DIR_RPM}
accumulate and nothing prunes either, so a recipe that no longer builds but was
built once into this TMPDIR is still listed.

The reader is avocado_sbom/rpms.py. It reads the lead and the two headers and
never the payload, and needs neither rpm nor rpm-native. The contract is
schema/avocado-rpm-manifest-v1.schema.json: unknown keys are permitted, and
additions do not bump the major. The tests build their RPMs byte by byte, so
they need no build either:

  python3 -m unittest discover -s meta-avocado-sbom/tests -p 'test_*.py'
